        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The council sent a man to count the doors in our house.\\n\\n“Fire regulations,” he said, showing me a badge without a photograph.\\n\\nI followed him with a notebook. Front door, back door, kitchen, bathroom. He counted the cupboard beneath the stairs twice.\\n\\n“That’s only one door,” I said.\\n\\n“From this side.”\\n\\nUpstairs, he stopped outside my daughter’s bedroom.\\n\\n“Does this one still open?”\\n\\n“Of course.”\\n\\nHe made a mark on his clipboard.\\n\\n“Try not to let her close it tonight.”\\n\\nAfter he left, I checked his figures. Eleven doors. I counted ten.\\n\\nAt dinner, Lucy asked why the man had measured her.\\n\\n“He didn’t measure you.”\\n\\n“He did while you were getting his tea. He said I’d fit.”\\n\\nI took her to sleep in my room. At midnight, someone knocked inside the wardrobe.\\n\\nLucy sat up.\\n\\n“That’s not ours,” she whispered.\\n\\nThe knocking moved to the bedroom door.\\n\\nMy husband’s voice asked to come in.\\n\\nHe was asleep beside me. I could feel his breath against my wrist.\\n\\nThen the voice became mine.\\n\\n“Lucy, sweetheart. You’ll make us late.”\\n\\nSomething slid beneath the door: a council inspection form.\\n\\nTen boxes had been ticked.\\n\\nThe eleventh was labelled OCCUPIED.\\n\\nBelow it, in my daughter’s careful handwriting, someone had written: Opens outwards.\\n\\nThe hinges began to lift from the frame.\\n\\nI grabbed Lucy and shook my husband awake.\\n\\nHe looked at the door, then at the wardrobe.\\n\\n“Which one did he count twice?” he asked.\\n\\nBefore I could answer, he climbed into the wardrobe and pulled it shut.\\n\\nThe knocking stopped.\\n\\nIn the morning, there were nine doors.\"\n}",
